<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ayllu">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ayllu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ayllu is the traditional community of the Andes, especially among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a>: a network of families, often with a putative or fictive common ancestor, holding a territory in common. Ayllus predate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> conquest and still exist, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Under the Incas, largely self-sufficient ayllus relied on the state storehouses only in disasters such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-nino">El Niño</a> years. Members owed one another reciprocal help (ayni) and communal work (mink'a), and paid the mit'a, labour owed as tax to the Inca state and later to the Spanish viceroyalties.</p></div>
